WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:03.520
Speaker 1: All right, let's get back to the
phones last show before Thanksgiving, we're

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:06.330
going to hear from a bunch of different people.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:12.750
David Pakman dot com slash discord is the
place where you can contact me.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.820
Let's go right into it and see what's on people's
minds.

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:24.869
Let's go to Is it Bora or Burrow from Miami,
Florida?

00:00:24.869 --> 00:00:28.780
Bora, from Miami, you're on the air.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:33.590
Please unmute and you'll be able to talk to
me.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:42.150
And last chance for Bora from Miami, Florida,
last opportunity to unmute yourself.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:43.760
And that's too bad.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.579
Let's go to Matt from Northern California.

00:00:45.579 --> 00:00:47.079
Matt, you're on the air next.

00:00:47.079 --> 00:00:50.080
Speaker 2: Hey, check one two.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:51.080
David, can you hear me?

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:52.379
Speaker 1: You're coming in loud and clear,
Matt.

00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:55.489
Speaker 2: David, thanks, man, I appreciate
you taking the call.

00:00:55.489 --> 00:00:56.489
Pleasure.

00:00:56.489 --> 00:00:59.309
So as stated, I live in rural Northern California.

00:00:59.309 --> 00:01:01.539
We're about two hours north of San Francisco.

00:01:01.539 --> 00:01:04.559
Speaker 1: And so is that like Humboldt County
pot area

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:10.350
Speaker 2: just south, I'm part of the Emerald
Triangle, Mendocino County, actually, that

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.560
that'll be Meadow Humble and Trinity County.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:14.780
Got it.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:16.770
You know it a little bit.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:23.850
So from where I'm sitting, it's just this
really interesting nexus of rural sort of

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:30.189
urban resentment mixed in with this infusion
of kind of back to land.

00:01:30.189 --> 00:01:31.619
Berkeley liberal.

00:01:31.619 --> 00:01:32.619
Organic.

00:01:32.619 --> 00:01:37.330
Kind of a culture clash.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:44.950
And with that, the the specific political
moment we're in, I think, is this confusing

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:51.119
identity crisis for especially like rural
satellite counties of the Bay Area in Northern

00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:55.250
California, there's there's a lot of resentment
towards San Francisco.

00:01:55.250 --> 00:02:02.049
You know, the the rash of organized starts
recently, these the COVID lockdowns.

00:02:02.049 --> 00:02:09.590
But and also there's this kind of libertarian
common denominator in the resentment that's

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:14.029
despite sort of some trappings of maybe some
Trumpism.

00:02:14.029 --> 00:02:17.609
They're generally pro-gay marriage, they're
pro cannabis.

00:02:17.609 --> 00:02:21.530
But then sort of on the other political side,
they're pro firearm ownership.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:27.260
So, you know, I guess I'm just kind of curious
about your perspective, David, on what democratic

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:30.800
messaging can do to start letting these.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:37.180
I think there's a huge place in the Democratic
Party platform for a lot of these ideas, but

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:41.890
there are some sort of communication breakdown,
a disconnect, and I'd love to know your thoughts

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:43.830
on messaging.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.760
Have we given up on these populations?

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:51.790
Is there an approach that you can see an inroad
to get them considering the blue ticket?

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:57.040
Speaker 1: Well, I hate to say this because
it's such an important issue, but if you are,

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.370
you're talking about strategy, which is great.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:01.169
I love it.

00:03:01.169 --> 00:03:06.349
If we want to talk about strategy, you probably
don't want to attack guns and gun owners.

00:03:06.349 --> 00:03:12.239
I mean, it's crazy to say it, but as we've
looked at in a number of studies, when the

00:03:12.239 --> 00:03:17.909
topic of guns is a factor in elections, it
tends to be good for Republicans and bad for

00:03:17.909 --> 00:03:23.419
Democrats, and it serves as a disincentive
for people to vote for Democrats.

00:03:23.419 --> 00:03:24.620
So.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:29.139
I'm the ultimate pragmatist, I'm not saying
gun safety doesn't matter, I'm not saying

00:03:29.139 --> 00:03:33.269
we don't have a massive gun safety issue in
the U.S. we do.

00:03:33.269 --> 00:03:39.870
But after the Sandy Hook shooting with Barack
Obama being president had, the House and Senate

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:44.819
still weren't able to get universal background
checks passed even with like 88 or 90 percent

00:03:44.819 --> 00:03:46.709
American support for it.

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:50.260
That doesn't mean I'm saying we give up on
the issue at all.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:54.869
Maybe it means you deal with it at the state
level, or I'm not sure.

00:03:54.869 --> 00:03:59.139
But for the group you're talking about where
gun ownership is an issue.

00:03:59.139 --> 00:04:04.999
Don't alienate people by talking about guns
and making it central.

00:04:04.999 --> 00:04:07.739
Make economic issues central, etc.

00:04:07.739 --> 00:04:10.629
And again, I know people will be mad at me
and they'll say David.

00:04:10.629 --> 00:04:11.769
But the gun issue matters.

00:04:11.769 --> 00:04:17.629
I agree it matters, but I think it's bad strategy,
and it only serves to mobilize Republicans

00:04:17.629 --> 00:04:21.909
and others who might otherwise vote for Democrats
but don't want to because of the perception

00:04:21.909 --> 00:04:23.409
that they're hostile to guns.

00:04:23.409 --> 00:04:27.520
So I think that's how I would start making
inroads with the group that you're describing

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:28.520
that.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:32.410
Speaker 2: David, can I ask a follow up question?

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:35.490
Yes, of course.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:41.130
Regarding the specific issue of cannabis and
the Democratic Party, we've found this really

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:46.919
strange stalemate that's emerged regarding
the federal legalization of cannabis, and

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:54.509
recently my understanding is there has been
some talk on the Beltway of even Republicans

00:04:54.509 --> 00:04:56.880
starting to come out in support of the federal
legalization.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:57.880
Yes.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:02.310
And I guess I have a concern that where, you
know, the Democrats could have put that feather

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:08.259
in their cap and the cans gotten kicked long
enough that, you know, maybe maybe the Republicans

00:05:08.259 --> 00:05:11.830
will pick up the can and put that feather
in their cap.

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:13.580
So there's two
Speaker 1: sides to that.

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:14.580
There's two sides that.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.870
So you're rightly pointing out, Hey, you know
what?

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:21.770
Democrats could have done more on federal
marijuana, maybe not legalization, but at

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:26.159
least decriminalization before Republicans
start working on it and saying, We want that

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:27.159
too.

00:05:27.159 --> 00:05:31.100
The thing is, I don't know that without Republican
help, Democrats would really have been able

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:32.100
to get it done.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.050
So I actually am not sure about that.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:40.381
But I do think that increasingly at minimum,
decriminalization is is becoming more and

00:05:40.381 --> 00:05:41.979
more bipartisan, which is a great thing.

00:05:41.979 --> 00:05:43.360
I don't care who's in favor of it.

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:44.360
Let's get it done.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:47.150
Speaker 2: Thank you, David, I appreciate
your thoughts.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:48.479
Speaker 1: My pleasure.

00:05:48.479 --> 00:05:55.229
There's Matt from Northern California with
a very powerful phone call coming in.

00:05:55.229 --> 00:05:56.229
Let's go.

00:05:56.229 --> 00:05:58.150
Oh, I don't know.

00:05:58.150 --> 00:05:59.540
Let's try Mike from Massachusetts.

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:01.110
Mike, you're on the air.

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:04.330
Speaker 2: Hey, David, can hear me.

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:05.710
Yes, I can.

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:07.560
Glad to be back.

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:09.800
Called in last week, we had a good talk.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:16.461
I had a question regarding kind of like the
larger scheme, like not too much larger scheme,

00:06:16.461 --> 00:06:20.479
larger scope of like the future of the United
States.

00:06:20.479 --> 00:06:28.169
So we've seen over the last 20 years maybe
a little bit more that there is a slow but

00:06:28.169 --> 00:06:34.500
steady general trend towards not just like
diversification of the population, but also

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:40.150
just like it's like a little more younger
generations as they come up to be a be a voting

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:44.449
age, but tend to be more liberal, more progressive
than, yes, previous generations.

00:06:44.449 --> 00:06:47.100
And overall, they're slowly gaining ground.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:55.210
But what we've also seen is that as far as
that, as that builds up the hostility and

00:06:55.210 --> 00:07:00.879
extremity of, you know, the right wing and,
you know, maybe some people more willing to

00:07:00.879 --> 00:07:06.159
take action against what they perceive to
be unethical or immoral people and policies

00:07:06.159 --> 00:07:08.449
also seems to be strengthening as well.

00:07:08.449 --> 00:07:11.779
And I guess my question is, do you see a point?

00:07:11.779 --> 00:07:19.180
Where do you see a point where this small
but dangerous contingent of the right reaches

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:23.050
a point where they see they are ultimately
going to lose the culture war?

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:28.439
They're going to lose like the like the overall
where the country swings in terms of politics

00:07:28.439 --> 00:07:33.270
and just where they ultimately reach a point
where they just say we have to, at least in

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:36.960
their minds, we have to save the country and
actually take action like they've been talking

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:38.030
about for a while.

00:07:38.030 --> 00:07:40.080
When do you think there's a point coming of
that?

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:41.490
Speaker 1: I would go further.

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:47.240
I think it's already started, but for now,
there aren't that many of them that are just

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:49.770
willing to willy nilly go and be violent.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:53.809
Yet I think a lot of them are doing the violent
rhetoric.

00:07:53.809 --> 00:07:58.460
I think a lot of them see themselves as at
a certain point, we will have to go and become

00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:00.090
violent in the real world.

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:01.090
Some of them have.

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:04.539
But yeah, I think that it's an absolutely
legitimate concern.

00:08:04.539 --> 00:08:10.779
And when you look at long term demographic
trends, things are changing.

00:08:10.779 --> 00:08:17.360
And short of killing people, which I'm against
and think is disgusting, there's a question

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:21.290
as to how are they going to be able to resist
it or push back against it.

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:23.229
And I think they're going to become more radicalized.

00:08:23.229 --> 00:08:24.969
I think they're going to become more insular.

00:08:24.969 --> 00:08:27.169
A lot of this rhetoric has gone totally mainstream.

00:08:27.169 --> 00:08:32.140
I mean, remember the clips of Tucker Carlson
saying on Fox News immigrants make the country

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:34.959
dirtier and poorer and stuff like that.

00:08:34.959 --> 00:08:35.959
So.

00:08:35.959 --> 00:08:41.430
Yes, I don't think it's hyperbolic to be worried
that they're going to go even crazier as these

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:43.789
changes continue to happen in the United States.

00:08:43.789 --> 00:08:45.089
And it's very scary.

00:08:45.089 --> 00:08:48.709
Speaker 2: So a follow up question, if I may.

00:08:48.709 --> 00:08:56.050
So we've seen a couple countries that used
to be solid democracies and now are only ostensibly

00:08:56.050 --> 00:08:59.230
democracies and essentially are autocracies.

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:02.050
The two prime examples in my mind right now
are.

00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:06.399
And maybe there's more, but the two prime
are Poland and Belarus.

00:09:06.399 --> 00:09:09.149
Now, I don't know the injury.

00:09:09.149 --> 00:09:14.130
I don't know the careful details of their
constitutions, what kind of checks and balances

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:18.620
they ever had in place and whatnot, obviously,
that those could cause differences.

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:21.920
But I mean, do you see?

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:25.750
Do you see something like what's happened
in Poland?

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:28.620
Theoretically possible here in the United
States?

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:30.330
Well, the
Speaker 1: degree to which it's

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:33.480
Speaker 2: happened, I think part, I
Speaker 1: think Belarus maybe is a better

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:35.980
example, but it's not.

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:41.050
It's not a hypothetical in the sense that
the U.S. for the first time a few days ago,

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:48.060
we covered this story on Tuesday was added
to the list of backsliding democracies for

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:49.060
the first time ever.

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:52.910
Speaker 2: Oh, I missed that story, but it's
disconcerting to know it's happening.

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:55.360
Speaker 1: It's happening now, unfortunately.

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:57.260
And what we mean by backsliding.

00:09:57.260 --> 00:09:59.870
Democracy is a slide towards authoritarianism,
so it's not.

00:09:59.870 --> 00:10:01.560
Sadly, it's not a hypothetical.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:02.560
It's actually happening.

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:04.210
And the question is, how far will it go?

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:07.600
Obviously, I hope it doesn't go as far as
it's gone in Belarus.

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:11.220
But that is that is happening already and
it's horrifying.

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:16.610
Speaker 2: One more, and then I promise I'll
let you move on, I know you got a lot of people

00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:17.940
waiting.

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:20.850
If that were to happen, do you see?

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:27.970
Do you see a possibility where the United
States, as in the 50 states, no longer exists

00:10:27.970 --> 00:10:28.970
down the road?

00:10:28.970 --> 00:10:31.930
And I don't mean like they've torn, they've
torn themselves apart from war?

00:10:31.930 --> 00:10:36.630
I mean, if something like what happens in
Belarus, as you just said, possibly happens

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:41.399
here and we continue down this backsliding
democracy path.

00:10:41.399 --> 00:10:47.370
Do you think that certain states that like,
say, New England and the Northeast, or maybe

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:53.610
the West Coast attempt to leave and get away
from this backsliding democracy?

00:10:53.610 --> 00:11:00.389
Or do you think that the OR do you think that
whoever is trying to take control in an authoritarian

00:11:00.389 --> 00:11:03.040
kind of way forces their hand to stay?

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:08.590
Speaker 1: You know, my view on that is these
are essentially random guesses.

00:11:08.590 --> 00:11:13.480
Humans are so bad at making that type of prediction,
which is why why I say, you know, when Trump

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:18.410
would say at the State of the Union, the US
will never be a socialist country, never is

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:20.200
a really long time.

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:26.519
And historically nations fail things that
are completely inconceivable take place.

00:11:26.519 --> 00:11:31.260
I have absolutely no idea on that, and I'm
not going to pretend to really have an educated

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:34.339
guess because I think we just don't know anything's
possible.

00:11:34.339 --> 00:11:37.019
Speaker 2: OK, fair enough.

00:11:37.019 --> 00:11:41.190
I appreciate your time, I'm sure a lot of
people are waiting to call in, so I'm sure

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:43.270
I'll be calling again and have a good Thanksgiving.

00:11:43.270 --> 00:11:44.270
Speaker 1: All right.

00:11:44.270 --> 00:11:45.270
Thanks, Mike.

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:47.709
Likewise, Mike from Massachusetts with a number
of important questions.

